H: Duluth Model: Gendered Framework and Subsequent Doubt by Creator
T: Systemic Response
C: USA
D: 1981
O: Ellen Pence & Michael Paymar
B: In 1981 in Duluth, Minnesota, what is known as the most influential model for identifying domestic violence was created by Ellen Pence and Michael Paymar. The famous duluth model gendered all perpetrators as male and all victims as female. The creators had no qualification in sociology and psychology or any related fields, they had Bachelor's degree in Arts. In 1999, Pence even doubted her own model stating '... [the model] did not fit the lived experience of many of the men and women we were working for.... Many of the men I[Pence] interviewed did not seem to articulate  a desire for power of their partner. Eventually we realized we were finding what we had already predetermined to find.'
U: blank
---
H: 2022 Dating App Survey: Online and In-Person Abuse Rates by Gender and Orientation
T: Online Violence
C: Australia
D: 2022
O: Australian Government
B: A survey in 2022 with a sample of 10k Australian adults who had used dating apps found that, 61.5% heterosexual men, 79.1% heterosexual women, 79.2% LGB+ men and 86.6% LGB+ women had experienced online abuse. 
A total of 26.6% heterosexual men, 35.4% heterosexual women, 45.5% LGB+ men and 49.5% LGB+ women reported experiencig in person abuse from someone from dating app.
12.3% heterosexual men and 12.8% heterosexual women had someone loiter around, follow or show up at their home, school or workplace.
In the category of online abuse: 39% heterosexual men, 36% heterosexual women, 47% LGB+ men and 46% LGB+ women victims reported that their perpetrators unmatched, blocked or deleted their account to remove conversation history after committing an incident of abuse. 
In category of In person abuse, it was 54,42,55,50 percent respectively.

Breakdown by different types of abuse:(Percentage of Heterosexual men, Heterosexual Women, LGB+ Men, LGB+ Women respectively)

a) Any Online Sexual Harassment: 57,77,78,85
b) Pressured to send a sexually explicit message: 27,43,52,61
c) Pressured to send a sexually explicit photo or video: 26,42,53,60
d) Continuing to be contacted after saying they were not interested: 42,49,53,61
e) Received an unwanted sexually explicit message: 30,58,53,72
f) Received an unwanted sexually explicit image or video: 28,49,53,60
g) Experienced any online stalking: 26,26,33,36
h) Pressured to provide information on their location or schedule: 23,24,30,34
i) Someone logged into their dating app without their permission: 14,7,16,13
j) Any abusive or threatening language: 38,47,53,60
k) Threatened in any way: 16,17,27,29
l) Had offensive comments posted about them online: 16,11,21,19
m) Was threatened with having their sexually explicit images shared: 13,10,20,21
n) Had sexually explicit images shared without consent: 12,8,19,17
o) Had images taken of sexual acts without knowledge: 11,8,18,14
p) Experienced any sexual assault or coercion: 21.2,27.9, 36.3, 42.1
q) Had someone attempt at sex act when not given consent: 13,14,23,27
r) Was verbally pressured to perform unwanted sexual acts: 16,23,31,36
s) Had someone threaten, or use physical force to recieve sexual acts: 12,11,21,20
t) Had their drink spiked to coerce them to perform unwanted sexual acts: 10,7,16,15
u) Experienced any reproductive abuse: 15.2,17.7, 31.8, 27
v) Had someone remove a condom during intercourse without consent, or refused to wear a condom: 11,14,24,21
w) Were lied to about sexual health status: 12,10,24,17
U: https://www.aic.gov.au/publications/rr/rr25
