H: Bilaterally Violent Relationships Cause More Injury Than Unilateral Male-Perpetrated Violence
T: Domestic Violence
C: USA
D: 2007
O: NIH
B: Data shows bilaterally violent relationships cause more injury than unilaterally male-perpetrated violence. In bilateral violence, injury rates are 31.4% for men against women and 25.3% for women against men. In unilateral violence, injury rates are 20% for men against women and 8.1% for women against men. This implies focus on 'severe male violence' ignores bilateral violence, even though severe male violence makes up only about 5% of all partner abuse.
U: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17395835/
