H: Flawed 'Intimate Terrorism' Model Challenged by Larger Student Sample
T: Domestic Violence
C: USA
D: 1995
O: Michael Johnson/Murray Straus
B: Prof Michael Johnson in 1995 coined 'Intimate Terrorism', he believed, men's abuse is different due to power, control, intent and intimidation. It was found that his results were based on data from men in domestic violence courts and abused women recruited from shelters. He was criticized by his peers for this flawed empirical data. Using a much larger sample of 13,877 university students Murray Straus found that 24% of male and 31.4% of female students reported perpetrating an act of physical violence. The data breakdown also revealed situational violence(violence arising due to a specific scenario without a desire to control their partner) was the most common form of partner violence. Mutual intimate terrorism (where both partner attempts to control each other via violence) was most common form of intimate terrorism. Little difference was found in percentage of sole intimate terrorists (where only one partner attempts to control the other via violence). 

The study further noted that women are indeed much more likely to be injured than men in unilateral partner violence. However, in mutual/bidirectional violence, injury rate is 5x as higher with little difference between men and women.
U: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10896-013-9560-7
---
